    description: "Follow-up to INV-rikis (PR #3189). docs/CONCEPTS.md now flags 1 ghost: `main_guard`. Investigation:\n\n- entrypoints.py:639 consumes `main_guard` via `concept_type == \"main_guard\"`.\n- packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py:1837 **programmatically emits** `main_guard` when its AST walker detects the `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` idiom: `module_meta = {\"concepts\": [{\"concept\": \"main_guard\", \"framework\": \"python\"}]}`.\n\n`scripts/generate-concepts`'s `scan_producers` only scans framework YAMLs — it never looks at Python source for literal `{\"concept\": \"X\"}` emissions. So `main_guard` (and any other programmatically-emitted concept added later) will always surface as `ghost` even though it has a real producer in production.\n\n**Scope:** add a second producer scan over hypergumbo-core / hypergumbo-lang-* source trees. Match patterns like `{\"concept\": \"X\"`, `('concept', 'X')`, and any other dict-literal form used in the code. The result: `main_guard` flips ghost → live with py.py as the producer.\n\n**Out of scope:** more exotic emitter forms (dynamic concept strings built at runtime). If a concept name is assembled from variables, the scanner will miss it — that's acceptable until we have evidence of such emitters existing.\n\n**Acceptance:**\n- docs/CONCEPTS.md's ghost count drops from 1 to 0.\n- `main_guard` row lists `py.py` or similar as producer.\n- Add tests similar to tests/test_generate_concepts.py::TestExtractConsumersFromText but for scan_producers. Cover: dict-literal emission with string key, with string concept value, nested in larger dicts, and negative cases (dynamic concept names via f-strings are NOT caught, document that explicitly).\n\n**Why P4:** the blind spot affects one concept today and the mis-classification is benign (ghost is noisier than inert but both mean \"needs attention\"). INV-rikis tackled the much larger ~30-concept blind spot; this cleanup can wait."  # 70f6
    fields: {}  # 70f6
